<section style="padding:72px 24px 40px;"><div style="max-width:860px;margin:0 auto;"><div class="rich"><p>Fencing in Garland, Texas, mostly comes down to two kinds of yards: older neighborhoods east and northeast of Dallas where a wood fence has stood 15 or 20 years and is leaning, going grey and dropping pickets, and newer east-side subdivisions toward Lake Ray Hubbard where the lot has no fence yet. Either way, the first job is the same: find out what the City of Garland and your HOA allow, then compare written quotes.</p><p>White Rock Fence Co is an advertising site. It does not build or repair fences. It connects Garland homeowners with independent fence contractors, so every price below is attributed to the firm that published it and every rule is attributed to the city. Call (214) 555-0137 or email info@whiterockfenceco.com to start.</p></div></div></section>
<section style="padding:0 24px 64px;"><div style="max-width:1100px;margin:0 auto;">
    <span class="kicker">What We Do in Garland</span>
    <h2 class="section-heading" style="font-size:clamp(22px,3vw,32px);margin:0 0 28px;">Services for Garland Properties</h2>
    <div class="feat-grid" style="display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(2,1fr);gap:20px;">
      <div style="background:#fff;border:1px solid #E3DFD8;border-radius:10px;padding:26px;box-shadow:0 2px 8px rgba(14,77,110,0.05);">
        <h3 style="font-family:'Inter',sans-serif;font-size:18px;font-weight:800;color:#2A3035;margin:0 0 8px;">Wood privacy fence installation</h3>
        <p style="font-size:14.5px;line-height:1.7;color:#555;margin:0;">Cedar or pine privacy fences for Garland back yards, including board-on-board. Garland's fence page allows up to eight feet in a residential area, so height is something to settle with the contractor, the City and your HOA.</p>
      </div>
      <div style="background:#fff;border:1px solid #E3DFD8;border-radius:10px;padding:26px;box-shadow:0 2px 8px rgba(14,77,110,0.05);">
        <h3 style="font-family:'Inter',sans-serif;font-size:18px;font-weight:800;color:#2A3035;margin:0 0 8px;">Fence repair</h3>
        <p style="font-size:14.5px;line-height:1.7;color:#555;margin:0;">Leaning sections, rotted posts, missing pickets and storm damage on fences that have stood 15 or 20 years. An independent contractor can tell you in writing whether repair or replacement makes more sense.</p>
      </div>
      <div style="background:#fff;border:1px solid #E3DFD8;border-radius:10px;padding:26px;box-shadow:0 2px 8px rgba(14,77,110,0.05);">
        <h3 style="font-family:'Inter',sans-serif;font-size:18px;font-weight:800;color:#2A3035;margin:0 0 8px;">Gate repair and automatic gates</h3>
        <p style="font-size:14.5px;line-height:1.7;color:#555;margin:0;">Sagging walk gates, dragging drive gates and openers that stopped responding. Power wiring for an opener is electrical work, so ask who holds the electrical license.</p>
      </div>
      <div style="background:#fff;border:1px solid #E3DFD8;border-radius:10px;padding:26px;box-shadow:0 2px 8px rgba(14,77,110,0.05);">
        <h3 style="font-family:'Inter',sans-serif;font-size:18px;font-weight:800;color:#2A3035;margin:0 0 8px;">Wrought iron fences</h3>
        <p style="font-size:14.5px;line-height:1.7;color:#555;margin:0;">Ornamental iron or steel for front and side yards. Garland's page limits a front-yard wood or wrought iron fence to 3.5 feet with open spacing, and any front-yard fence needs a permit.</p>
      </div>
</div>
